+**Formulas:**
+*   **Gradient Strength:** $g(x) = |\nabla f(x)|$, where $f(x)$ is the density at voxel $x$.
+*   **Interval Width:** The width $w$ of the chosen density interval is a linear function of the gradient strength $g$ to obtain a constant width of the (thick) surface: $w \propto g$ [S2, book].
 
 **Parameters:**
-- `num_bins`: Number of histogram bins. Typical value: 256 [S1, unofficial].
-- `threshold`: Fraction of max gradient histogram value for peak detection. Typical value: 0.1 [S1, unofficial].
-- `interval_width`: Width of the density interval around peaks. Can be fixed or proportional to gradient strength [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Histogram Bin Size:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Gradient Threshold:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Fuzzy Membership Function Shape:** Not given in the sources (typically Gaussian or triangular).
 
 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- **Noise:** The method works well only if data are relatively noise-free [S1, unofficial]. Noise can create spurious peaks in the gradient histogram.
-- **Overlapping Materials:** Works well only if density ranges of features are well isolated [S1, unofficial]. If materials have similar densities, differentiation is difficult.
-- **Many Materials:** Works well only if not many distinct materials (e.g., bone, fat, skin) are present [S1, unofficial]. Complex scenes with many overlapping materials may require more sophisticated analysis.
-- **Derivative Calculation:** The accuracy of the gradient magnitude depends on the derivative computation method. Sobel operators are commonly used but may be sensitive to noise [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Noise Sensitivity:** The method works well only if the data are relatively noise-free. Noise can create spurious peaks in the derivative histogram [S1, unofficial][S2, book].
+*   **Feature Isolation:** It requires that the density ranges of the features are well isolated. Overlapping density ranges can lead to ambiguous classification [S1, unofficial][S2, book].
+*   **Material Complexity:** It performs best when there are few distinct materials (e.g., bone, fat, skin). A large number of distinct materials can complicate the histogram analysis and peak identification [S1, unofficial][S2, book].
